---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc270.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Integral View of Interim Periods" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Estimated Annual Effective Tax Rate" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Cost Smoothing Conventions Unique to the Integral View" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Minimum Required Interim Disclosures" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Seasonal Business Disclosure" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Interim Goodwill Impairment – Cross-Reference" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example – Estimated Annual Effective Tax Rate" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Worked Example – Integral-View Cost Anticipation" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s13", label: "ASC 270 vs IAS 34 – Integral vs Discrete View" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Glossary" },
        { id: "history", label: "History & Development" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Timely information at the cost of some measurement precision</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Interim reporting trades off between TIMELINESS (a key enhancing qualitative characteristic) and the greater precision available only once a full annual period's activity is known. ASC 270 governs how entities balance that trade-off – determining which measurement conventions carry forward from annual reporting, and which are adapted specifically for the shorter interim period.</div></div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">A genuinely different underlying philosophy from IFRS</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 270 is built around the "INTEGRAL" view of interim periods – treating each interim period as an integral PART of the annual period, rather than a wholly independent reporting period in its own right. This is the single most consequential conceptual difference between US GAAP and IFRS interim reporting (Section 13), producing genuinely different cost recognition patterns within the year for economically identical situations.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Primarily a public-company requirement</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 270 applies to interim financial information for periods shorter than a full fiscal year – most significantly, the quarterly (10-Q) filings SEC-registered public companies are required to make. Private companies are not typically required to prepare interim financial statements under US GAAP, though many do so voluntarily for internal management or lender purposes.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Integral View of Interim Periods</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Interim results anticipate the full year, where appropriate</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Under the integral view, certain costs and revenues that benefit or relate to the FULL fiscal year may be ALLOCATED across interim periods on a basis consistent with anticipated full-year activity, rather than recognised strictly as-incurred within each individual quarter. Historically this has permitted smoothing conventions for items such as anticipated volume-based cost variances expected to be absorbed by year end, or costs of a benefit clearly spanning the full year – a fundamentally different STARTING PHILOSOPHY from IAS 34's discrete-period orientation.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Estimated Annual Effective Tax Rate</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Applied to year-to-date interim pretax income</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Interim income tax expense is computed using the entity's BEST ESTIMATE of the ANNUAL EFFECTIVE TAX RATE, applied to year-to-date pretax income – NOT the actual tax computed as if the interim period were a standalone annual period. This estimated-rate approach is, notably, ALSO used under IAS 34 – one of the more closely converged specific mechanics between the two interim-reporting frameworks, despite their differing overall philosophies.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Cost Smoothing Conventions Unique to the Integral View</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Where US GAAP and IFRS genuinely part ways</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 270 permits certain INTEGRAL-period conventions with no direct IAS 34 counterpart – for example, historically anticipating and spreading the cost of a planned MAJOR ANNUAL MAINTENANCE shutdown, or deferring interim standard-cost variances expected to be ABSORBED by year end, so no single quarter bears a disproportionate share of a cost genuinely tied to full-year operations. IAS 34, by contrast, generally requires each interim period's recognition and measurement to follow the SAME principles that would apply if that period were a standalone annual period – meaning IAS 34 does NOT permit accruing or deferring costs purely for interim smoothing purposes unless the definition of a liability or asset is independently satisfied at that date.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Minimum Required Interim Disclosures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A defined minimum content set</div><div class="check-list"><span>Sales/revenues, provision for income taxes, and net income</span><span>Basic and diluted EPS</span><span>Seasonal revenues, costs, or expenses</span><span>Significant changes in estimates or provisions for income taxes</span><span>Disposal of a component of an entity and unusual/infrequent items</span><span>Contingent items</span><span>Changes in accounting principles or estimates</span><span>Significant changes in financial position</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Seasonal Business Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Warning users against extrapolating a single quarter</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Entities whose business is SEASONAL in nature are encouraged (and, for material seasonality, effectively required) to disclose the seasonal nature of their activities, and to consider supplementing interim reports with information on twelve-month-to-date or last-twelve-months results, to avoid users mistakenly EXTRAPOLATING a single, unrepresentative quarter into a full-year expectation. IAS 34 imposes a broadly similar seasonal-disclosure expectation.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Interim Goodwill Impairment – Cross-Reference</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Consistent with the interim-annual philosophy elsewhere</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Goodwill impairment losses recognised at an interim date are NOT reversed in a later interim or annual period, even if the underlying indicators subsequently improve – this specific principle is addressed within ASC 350 (Intangibles–Goodwill and Other) rather than ASC 270 itself, but reflects the SAME integral-period philosophy: an interim loss, once recognised, is treated with the same permanence as an annual-period loss would be. This closely parallels the IFRIC 10 conclusion reached under IFRS for the analogous fact pattern.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example – Estimated Annual Effective Tax Rate</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Applying a full-year rate estimate to Q2 results</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Q2 tax expense using the annual rate estimate</div><div class="wk-body">An entity estimates its full-year effective tax rate at 24%. Year-to-date pretax income through the end of Q2 is $8,000,000 (Q1 was $3,500,000, Q2 was $4,500,000). Q1 tax expense, using the then-current rate estimate of 25%, was $875,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Year-to-date tax expense through Q2 = $8,000,000 × 24% = $1,920,000. Q2 tax expense = $1,920,000 &minus; $875,000 (already recognised in Q1) = <strong>$1,045,000</strong> – a "catch-up" adjustment reflecting both Q2's own income AND the revised full-year rate estimate applied cumulatively.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example – Integral-View Cost Anticipation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A planned annual maintenance cost, spread across quarters</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Anticipated major overhaul</div><div class="wk-body">A manufacturer performs a major plant overhaul every June, expected to cost $2,400,000, providing a clear benefit across the ENTIRE fiscal year's production. Under the integral view, the entity spreads $600,000 of anticipated cost into each of Q1 through Q4, rather than recognising the full $2,400,000 entirely within Q2 when the overhaul actually occurs.</div><div class="wk-answer">Under US GAAP's integral approach, this SMOOTHING may be considered acceptable, given the clear full-year benefit. Under IAS 34's discrete view, by contrast, the FULL $2,400,000 would generally need to be recognised in Q2 alone, when the cost is actually incurred and no independent asset or liability existed at the end of Q1 to justify an earlier accrual – illustrating the practical, numerical consequence of the two frameworks' differing interim philosophies for the exact same underlying fact pattern.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Summary reference (see Section 6 for the full minimum list)</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Beyond the minimum content items in Section 6, PUBLIC entities are also subject to SEC Regulation S-X and Article 10 requirements layered on top of ASC 270's own baseline, including comparative prior-year interim figures and specific management's discussion and analysis content – a further public-company-specific disclosure layer without an equally detailed IFRS regulatory parallel embedded directly within IAS 34 itself.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a specific cost genuinely qualifies for integral-view smoothing, versus requiring discrete as-incurred recognition</span><span>Identifying material seasonal patterns warranting disclosure</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Best estimate of the full-year effective tax rate, reassessed and trued up each interim period</span><span>Anticipated full-year activity levels underlying any integral-period cost allocations</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>ASC 270 vs IAS 34 – Integral vs Discrete View</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – ASC 270 vs IAS 34</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span>US GAAP (ASC 270)</span><span>IFRS (IAS 34)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Underlying philosophy</span><span>INTEGRAL – each interim period is part of the annual period</span><span>DISCRETE – each interim period stands on its own, applying annual-equivalent recognition principles</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Cost smoothing across interim periods</span><span>Permitted in defined circumstances (Section 5)</span><span>Generally NOT permitted purely for smoothing – recognition follows the same tests as annual reporting</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Estimated annual effective tax rate</span><span>Required</span><span>Also required – genuinely converged on this specific mechanic</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Minimum disclosure format</span><span>Condensed statements plus a defined minimum item list</span><span>Condensed statements plus a broadly similar minimum item list – closely aligned in content, if not in underlying philosophy</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">The integral-vs-discrete distinction is the classic, most heavily emphasised US GAAP/IFRS interim reporting difference in comparative professional curricula – even though, on tax computation mechanics specifically, the two frameworks are quite closely aligned.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 270 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Interim income tax expense</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Computed using the estimated annual effective rate applied to year-to-date income</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Integral-view cost accrual/deferral</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Spread across quarters where the anticipated full-year benefit test is met</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Change in estimate identified mid-year</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised in the interim period identified, per ASC 250's prospective treatment</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 740</span><span>Income Taxes – supplies the general tax accounting framework the estimated annual rate method is layered on top of</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 250</span><span>Governs how changes in estimate identified during an interim period are subsequently treated</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 350</span><span>Supplies the no-reversal-of-interim-goodwill-impairment principle referenced in Section 8</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 280</span><span>Segment Reporting – public entities disclose segment information within interim reports as well as annual ones</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary preview</div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Integral view</div><div class="glossary-def">The US GAAP interim reporting philosophy treating each interim period as a component of the annual period, permitting certain cost-smoothing conventions.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Discrete view</div><div class="glossary-def">IAS 34's interim reporting philosophy, treating each interim period on a standalone basis with the same recognition and measurement principles as an annual period.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>

    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Condensed financial statements", definition: "Abbreviated interim financial statements presenting summarised line items rather than the full detail of annual statements, supplemented by the minimum required disclosures." },
      { term: "Estimated annual effective tax rate", definition: "Management's best estimate of the full-year effective tax rate, applied to year-to-date pretax income to compute interim income tax expense." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC270 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

